s taken out of service, across one such transition\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A continuous ping from node3 to node4 was run while transit link A (node1 eth1 / node2 vtnet1) was administratively downed; only a single packet (seq 20) was lost, OSPF neighbor/route state confirmed traffic shifted fully to transit B during the outage, and after transit A was restored OSPF re-adjacenced (Full/DR) and ECMP routing to both paths was re-confirmed — a directly observed healthy-baseline -> disruption -> recovery transition.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-cee265633b2b41c9\",\"cmd-fbd55d401c88450e\",\"cmd-90369aff72554f47\",\"cmd-3d63536d1f6a4976\",\"cmd-6f9fbabe6dee4cd8\",\"cmd-ce3a7e2666fc424c\",\"cmd-a6811f5b234c4ef1\",\"cmd-c31fffd1d6eb422c\",\"cmd-7473df3219eb4aa8\",\"cmd-900740f06c5840e4\"]}],\"confidence\":0.85,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.88,\"summary\":\"Executor mutations were tightly scoped to the task: interface down/up flaps on node1/node2/node3/node4 for MAC/bridge-ID topology discovery (cmd-86ee11cd8c684e0a, cmd-67575e2598594ba3, cmd-0eef5e7907c84b62, cmd-bbf54dad2adb4f63, cmd-56c2e77163094c25) were bounded (seconds) and immediately restored, serving a clear discovery prerequisite. tcpdump capture files on node1 were cleaned up implicitly by the later node1 reboot (no cap_*.log residue in after-executor snapshot). One command (cmd-6262d8c7bc0143aa, editing /etc/rc.conf.d/frr and starting frr) hit a 120s proxy timeout but the subsequent command (cmd-b407936554ca419b) confirms it had actually succeeded (frr running) — not a failed mutation, just an unconfirmed-result artifact. Minor unreverted residue: OPNsense config.xml.bak/.bak2/.bak3 backup copies and temporary python helper scripts (addifs.py, addrules.py, setospf.py) were left in /conf and /tmp respectively on node2, though the reboot-triggered /tmp clear on node2 appears to have removed the scripts (not present in after-executor temp-file listing); the config.xml backups likely remain in /conf but are low-risk, standard defensive copies rather than damaging residue. No failed/conflicting services, no weakened security controls, and no destructive or broad mutations were observed.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-86ee11cd8c684e0a\",\"cmd-67575e2598594ba3\",\"cmd-0eef5e7907c84b62\",\"cmd-bbf54dad2adb4f63\",\"cmd-56c2e77163094c25\",\"cmd-6262d8c7bc0143aa\",\"cmd-b407936554ca419b\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All material outcomes of the task instruction are directly satisfied by observed evidence: the dual-transit topology was correctly mapped, OSPF/ECMP redundant routing was configured and shown operational over both transit links, node3 and node4 achieved bidirectional reachability, and a live transit-link outage plus recovery was directly demonstrated with only a single dropped ping and confirmed OSPF path withdrawal/re-convergence. Independently of the task contract, the executor also ran the provider-wide restart protocol on all four nodes, each showing a pre-restart baseline, a restart acknowledgement (SSH/exec timeouts during the gap), a changed boot identity (VyOS boot_id, OPNsense kern.boottime, RHEL boot_id), and recovered OSPF/routing/ping state afterward — this evidence is coherent and complete for all four nodes. One caveat: node2's before_prepare boot-identity snapshot (kern.boottime ~Jun 15) differs from the 'baseline' boottime captured just before its deliberate restart (~06:01:59 same day as the session), implying node2 underwent an earlier, undocumented boot-identity change sometime between session start and the deliberate restart demonstration — most likely an artifact of first-boot-from-template rather than an executor action, but this is not conclusively established from the trace.\",\"limitations\":[\"The apparent earlier, undocumented boot-identity change on node2 (kern.boottime jumping from a Jun 15 value to a Sep 6 06:01:59 value before the deliber